Body:
Great work on this PR! The approach of routing OAuth callbacks through the main server is exactly what's needed for constrained environments.

  I want to highlight an important use case and a blocker I found that affects both this PR and #21385 (already merged).

  The architecture: Consider a deployment where a frontend web app runs in one pod (public-facing), and OpenCode runs in another pod (opencode serve) that is only reachable from the
  internal network. The user interacts with the frontend in the browser, and the frontend communicates with OpenCode over REST.

  The intended OAuth flow:

  1. Frontend calls POST /mcp/:name/auth → gets { authorizationUrl, oauthState }
  2. Frontend opens authorizationUrl in the user's browser
  3. User authorizes → OAuth provider redirects to the frontend's public URL (configured via redirectUri from #21385)
  4. Frontend receives the callback, verifies state === oauthState
  5. Frontend calls POST /mcp/:name/auth/callback with { code } → OpenCode completes the token exchange

  The endpoints for this flow already exist on dev. The redirectUri config from #21385 tells the OAuth provider where to redirect.

  The blocker: startAuth unconditionally calls McpOAuthCallback.ensureRunning(oauthConfig?.redirectUri) (index.ts:727), which tries to start a local HTTP server on the port parsed
  from the redirect URI. When redirectUri is something like https://frontend.example.com/oauth/callback, parseRedirectUri returns port 443, and server.listen(443) fails with EACCES
  on any non-root process. This crashes startAuth before the OAuth flow even begins.

  Neither #21385 nor this PR modifies ensureRunning to handle external redirect URIs.

  The fix is small — skip the local callback server when the redirect host isn't localhost:

```js
  export async function ensureRunning(redirectUri?: string): Promise<void> {
      if (redirectUri) {
        try {
          const url = new URL(redirectUri)
          if (url.hostname !== "127.0.0.1" && url.hostname !== "localhost") {
            log.info("redirect URI is external, skipping local callback server", { redirectUri })
            return
          }
        } catch {}
      }

      const { port, path } = parseRedirectUri(redirectUri)
      // ... rest unchanged
  }
```

  @lumincui would you be able to include this fix in this PR? It should be a small addition to ensureRunning in oauth-callback.ts.
